WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.567
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.690
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.830
to apply in your cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:06.600
I'm Priyanka.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.940
And in this episode,
I am pleased to have

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.670
Jen Person back with us to
talk about another mobile app

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.745
backend service solution.

00:00:13.745 --> 00:00:16.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:24.640
Good morning, Jen.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.682
Welcome back to "Get
Cooking in Cloud."

00:00:26.682 --> 00:00:27.890
JEN PERSON: Thanks, Priyanka.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.310
I'm super excited to
share today's solution.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:31.720
I think it's a real winner.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.928
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So last
time you were on the show,

00:00:33.928 --> 00:00:36.040
you left us on a bit
of a cliffhanger.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:38.320
You shared a mobile app
backend service solution

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.500
using Firebase and App
Engine standard environment.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.560
My app, Priyanka's
Kitchen, uses avatars

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.720
in cloud storage for Firebase.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.760
I need a way to create thumbnail
versions of larger images.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.740
I'll need to be able to
download the full-size image,

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.320
scale it, download
the scaled version,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.490
and then write back
to cloud storage.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:02.050
That means I will need to
write to the file system.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:05.260
But so far, you didn't recommend
the recipe for backend services

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.320
that call native binaries,
write to the file system,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.300
or make other system calls.

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.832
I am really excited to
see that solution today.

00:01:12.832 --> 00:01:14.290
JEN PERSON: That's
right, Priyanka.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:15.900
In an earlier
episode, I explained

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.400
that Firebase solves a lot
of the mobile app backend

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.480
problems.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:21.730
And last episode, we saw
that adding App Engine

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.190
standard environment
provides a backend service

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.830
to modify the synchronized
data, filling a gap

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:28.670
in the Firebase-only solution.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.510
But as you pointed out, App
Engine standard environment

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.400
cannot solve every problem.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:34.960
For your image
thumbnail example,

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.270
we definitely need a
different solution.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:38.767
Are you ready to see it?

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.850
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I am
waiting with bated breath.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.020
Let's cook it up.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:46.750
JEN PERSON: So again, here's our
recipe from the last episode.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.760
And here's today's recipe.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:49.690
That's right.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.700
In this design
pattern, we still use

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.320
Firebase for all its mobile
app backend benefits.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.770
But instead of App Engine
standard environment,

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.223
we use App Engine
flexible environment.

00:01:59.223 --> 00:02:01.390
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I notice
that the ingredient here

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.230
is the same--

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:03.460
App Engine.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.310
And this looks very similar
to last episode's recipe,

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:08.539
but it differs in an
important way, right?

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:09.257
JEN PERSON: Yes.

00:02:09.257 --> 00:02:11.590
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Like App
Engine standard environment,

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:13.390
App Engine flexible
environment is

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.090
an app platform that
monitors, updates, and scales

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:17.750
the hosting environment.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.890
All you need to do is write your
mobile backend service code.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.930
Those features are the same.

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.290
Where App Engine standard and
flexible environment differ

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.440
is that flexible environment
runs your backend service

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.850
inside a Docker container
that you can configure.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.210
JEN PERSON: Exactly.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.820
And this means that you
can call native binaries,

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.790
write to the file system,
and make other system calls--

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:42.040
all the things you
can't do an App Engine

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.820
standard environment.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:46.720
So if your app needs to process
user data or orchestrate

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.440
events, extending Firebase with
App Engine flexible environment

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.290
gives you automatic real-time
data synchronization

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.710
without needing to run your code
inside the App Engine sandbox.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.810
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: So
this solution is obviously

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.610
a bit more flexible.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:01.400
JEN PERSON: Yep.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.180
As the name implies, the App
Engine flexible environment

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.000
will give you more flexibility.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.750
This is a great solution
for Firebase apps

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.690
that need a backend service to
modify the synchronized data.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.420
And that service requires a
custom server configuration

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.390
or third-party libraries that
aren't supported by App Engine

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:17.307
standard environments.

00:03:17.307 --> 00:03:19.265
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Are
there any other benefits

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:20.930
to the flexible environment?

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:23.020
JEN PERSON: There is one more.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.480
You may recall that the App
Engine standard environment

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:28.160
only holds a connection
open for two minutes.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.580
Well, App Engine
flexible environment

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.160
can hold a connection
open for 24 hours.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.370
So for example, you can
use this architecture

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.010
for backend services that
require a persistent connection

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:42.610
to Firebase to receive
notifications of data changes.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.220
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I can totally
see how that would be useful.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.178
JEN PERSON: Definitely.

00:03:46.178 --> 00:03:48.730
And since you can write
to the file system,

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.920
you can use this recipe
to generate thumbnails

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.315
for Priyanka's Kitchen.

00:03:52.315 --> 00:03:54.190
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Well,
that's all for today

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:55.390
on "Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:58.120
Jen, thank you so much
for coming on the show.

